    "1": {
      "_type": "Sofa",
      "sofaNum": 1,
      "sofaID": "_InitialView",
      "mimeType": "text",
      "sofaString": "9001\tPt, Avery Coleman, is a 5 year old female with a history of dilated cardiomyopathy referred by Dana Whitfield, RN, Transplant Coordinator, for Heart Transplant Psychosocial Assessment.  Information for this assessment was obtained through an interview with the parents and review of the medical records.  SW spent 50 minutes of face to face time with the family.  Living situation: Avery lives with her mother and father in Millbrook Hollow, Rivendale.  They reside in a two bedroom apartment with central heat and air conditioning.  There is no smoking in the home.  Support systems: the mother and father report leaning on each other and on extended family for support.  Avery is enrolled in kindergarten and the school has been notified of her medical condition.  Avery is covered by Rivendale State Medicaid.  The family owns a reliable vehicle and reports no difficulty getting Avery to appointments.  The family lives approximately four hours from the transplant center, which adds to travel cost for appointments.  Course of illness: Avery was diagnosed with dilated cardiomyopathy after a routine well-child visit revealed an irregular heartbeat.  She was started on standard heart failure medications and has since been listed for transplant evaluation.  Financial situation: the family reports finances are tight due to the cost of frequent travel for appointments.  The father continues to work full time and the mother has reduced her hours to manage caregiving.  Assessment and recommendations: the family demonstrates a good understanding of the transplant process and appears well bonded with Avery.  Recommendations: continue to monitor the family's travel-related financial burden and connect them with the hospital's travel assistance fund.\n\nJamie Ostrander, LCSW\nPediatric Transplant Team\n(000) 000-0000"
    }
  }
}